{
 "esm": "1.1.0",
 "metadata": {
  "name": "NoahFrozenSoilColumnMelt",
  "description": "A MELTING snow column of WRF's unified Noah land-surface model: the snow-surface energy balance, vertical water transport and the freeze/thaw phase change, mounted together, with the melt water carried from the first into the second and the sum asserted against WRF's own step. WHY A COUPLING DOCUMENT. Each of the three mounted components is pinned on its own: `snow_energy.esm` reproduces SNOPAC's closure and melt rate against a pre-limiter publisher, `soil_moisture.esm` reproduces SMFLX/SRT as Richards' equation, and `frozen_soil.esm` reproduces the freeze/thaw sink exactly. None of them, alone, says anything about the quantity a consumer integrates, which is the SUM of the last two driven by the first -- and WRF never forms that sum. SFLX runs SNOPAC, which adds the melt to the surface water (`PRCP1 = PRCP1 + EX`); then SMFLX, which advances the liquid water; then SHFLX -> HRT, whose sink acts on what SMFLX has already moved. WRF's answer is a sequential composition; a continuous formulation is the sum evaluated at ONE instantaneous state. WHAT A SNOW COLUMN ADDS THAT BARE GROUND CANNOT. On the bare-ground documents the surface supply is dew, of order 1e-9 m/s. Here it is snowmelt from a third mount, up to 8.0e-7 m/s -- two to three orders larger, and the dominant term in the liquid budget. It also brings in a routing distinction that does not exist on bare ground: NOPAC adds frost to the soil water, SNOPAC does not, because under a pack the frost is deposited on the SNOW. `dew_to_soil` = 0 carries that, and it is not cosmetic. THE THREE CLAIMS ARE UNCHANGED, and every assertion below is labelled with the one it serves. (1) LIQUID BUDGET -- transport + phase = WRF's total step: `dsh2o_dt_total`. Its new term is checked in its own right by `melt` (against SNOPAC's own melt rate) and `water_supply` (against SNOPAC's own PRCP1, which the assembly must reconstruct as precipitation plus melt and NOT plus frost). (2) TOTAL-WATER BUDGET -- transport alone reproduces WRF's `smc` step, because the phase change moves water between liquid and ice within a layer and does not change the total: `dsmc_dt_total` and `dsice_dt`. An error that leaked transport into the phase term passes (1) and fails (2). (3) THE SWITCHES, AT ZERO TOLERANCE -- `phase_active`, the frozen sink's clamp decision made at the assembly's shared state against WRF's at its post-SMFLX state, and `melting`, SNOPAC's own branch. (3) is what licenses (1): the phase rate depends on the liquid water only through that discrete switch, so a switch that does not flip cannot admit the splitting error. PRECONDITION, not a result: `supersaturation_active` = 0. SSTEP's redistribution is a post-step projection that no tendency can carry, and the sum is only meaningful while it is inactive. THE REFERENCE STEP IS A FLOOR CASE (FORTRAN_BUGS N99, PLAN section 6) and its V is the same one the bare-ground documents measured: the reference (sh2o_out - sh2o_in)/dt is a difference, so cancellation bounds it below, while above it WRF's interval clamp starts flipping. Over the snow-covered steps the clamp decisions at 1e-6 s and 0.01 s agree everywhere and the 60 s ones do not, so 0.01 s it is. SCOPE. `qtot` is consumer-supplied, as in `frozen_soil.esm`'s own tests, so this isolates the WATER budget; the heat budget behind qtot is the soil-heat component's and joins in a larger assembly. The UA_PHYS canopy-shading branch is off everywhere and is not transcribed. THIS COLUMN: case B step 1841, a 30 cm pack under warm advection, SNOPAC's MELT branch, and the strongest melt in the cold-season set -- ex = 7.976e-07 m/s, which is the entire surface water supply here because the precipitation falls as snow and goes to the pack. The frozen sink is held at `sh2o` in the top layer and runs in the other three.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "tags": [
   "wrf",
   "land-surface",
   "noah",
   "coupling",
   "snowmelt",
   "soil-moisture",
   "frozen-soil",
   "operator-splitting"
  ],
  "references": [
   {
    "citation": "Koren, V., Schaake, J., Mitchell, K., Duan, Q.-Y., Chen, F., & Baker, J. M. (1999). A parameterization of snowpack and frozen ground intended for NCEP weather and climate models. J. Geophys. Res., 104(D16), 19569-19585."
   },
   {
    "citation": "Ek, M. B., et al. (2003). Implementation of Noah land surface model advances in the NCEP operational mesoscale Eta model. J. Geophys. Res., 108(D22), 8851."
   },
   {
    "citation": "wrf-model/WRF v4.8.0 phys/module_sf_noahlsm.F: SNOPAC's PRCP1 = PRCP1 + EX, then SMFLX, then SHFLX -> HRT -> SNKSRC -- the sequential composition this document sums instead."
   }
  ]
 },
 "metaparameters": {
  "NLEV": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 4,
   "description": "Number of soil layers. Declared at document level and bound into both soil mount edges, so the two share one vertical grid."
  }
 },
 "models": {
  "Sno": {
   "ref": "../components/land_surface/noah/snow_energy.esm",
   "bindings": {
    "NLEV": "NLEV"
   },
   "expression_template_imports": [
